# Testing Rules & Conventions

## Test Tiers

- **Unit** — no external dependencies (no network, no database, no filesystem beyond
  temp dirs). Fast, isolated, deterministic.
- **Integration** — exercises real external services (database, cache, third-party API
  sandbox). May require setup/teardown fixtures.
- **E2E** — full system under test, driven through its public interface (HTTP, CLI,
  UI). Validates user-facing behavior end-to-end.

## Key Patterns

- Place tests in dedicated test files or directories that mirror the source tree.
- Use async test support when testing async code — do not block on futures/promises
  inside synchronous test bodies.
- Prefer real implementations over mocks. Mock only at boundaries you do not own
  (external APIs, system clocks, randomness).
- Use your language's temp-directory facilities for any test-created files. Never
  hardcode paths — they collide in parallel runs and break across platforms.
- Every bug fix must include a regression test that would have caught the bug.

## Test Quality

- No test may mutate global state. If global state is unavoidable, reset it in
  teardown so test ordering does not matter.
- No real network requests in unit tests. Stub or fake external endpoints.
- Test names describe the behavior being verified, not the method under test.
  Prefer `"returns empty list when no results match filter"` over `"test_search"`.
- Assertions must be specific. Assert on exact values, error types, or structure —
  not just truthiness. `assert result == expected` over `assert result`.
